  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through next Sunday. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will crest over the area tonight through\nWednesday, with winds becoming light and skies remaining mostly\nclear. This setup will be favorable for boundary layer\ndecoupling tonight, with low temperatures likely dropping down\nto around 20 or even the upper teens in the north, and low to\nmid 20s Downeast. By Thursday, the ridge will begin to shift\neastward, allowing for return flow out of the southwest to\nadvect warmer air into the region. Highs on Thursday will\nrebound, in fact soar above average for this time of the year,\nlifting into the lower 60s throughout the forecast area.\n\nAnother cold front will cross the forecast area early Friday\nmorning, and will again be mostly moisture starved. There is a\nchance for scattered showers along this boundary, however\nrainfall amounts are likely to remain below a tenth of an inch,\nwith no improvement to drought conditions.\n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal with \nNorthern Maine. Overall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index\nblend keeps the Northern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to \nSevere Dryness category with portions of the Upper St. John \nBasin now dipping into the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 25 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen with continued precipitation \ndeficits.",
